C = [
("h1", "7. Revelation 19: The Marriage of the Lamb"),
("p", "Claim: Revelation 19 announces the marriage of the Lamb to “his wife,” the wife of the prophets. The announcement follows the judgment of the great whore and comes before the rider on the white horse. The wife is arrayed in the two garments that the prophets give her, fine linen and righteousness. Those who are “called unto the marriage supper” are blessed, and they are the guests of the supper, not the wife. Nothing in the chapter gives the wife the name of the church or the Body."),
("sub", "7.1 Where the marriage stands"),
("p", "The chapter opens with a great voice in heaven over the fall of the harlot: “For true and righteous are his judgments: for he hath judged the great whore, which did corrupt the earth with her fornication, and hath avenged the blood of his servants at her hand” (Revelation 19:2). The elders and the four beasts worship, and then the voice of a great multitude, “as the voice of many waters, and as the voice of mighty thunderings,” cries, “Alleluia: for the Lord God omnipotent reigneth” (Revelation 19:6). The next verse is the announcement: “Let us be glad and rejoice, and give honour to him: for the marriage of the Lamb is come, and his wife hath made herself ready” (Revelation 19:7)."),
("p", "The order is the order of the prophets. In Ezekiel the harlot is judged and then the covenant is established (Ezekiel 16:38, 60). In Isaiah the forsaken wife is gathered after the small moment of wrath (Isaiah 54:7). In Revelation the judgment on the whore is followed at once by the marriage of the Lamb. The two women are set side by side, one judged and one made ready, and the chapter turns from the one to the other without a break. After the wedding the heaven is opened and the rider is named Faithful and True (Revelation 19:11), and the chapter goes on to the war."),
("sub", "7.2 “His wife”"),
("p", "The word is wife. It is not church, Body, saints or assembly. The marriage figure of the prophets uses the same word of Israel: “as a wife treacherously departeth from her husband” (Jeremiah 3:20), “a wife of youth” (Isaiah 54:6), “a wife that committeth adultery” (Ezekiel 16:32). The husband is now named the Lamb, and the wife is his. Revelation does not invent a second marriage. It reports that the marriage the prophets promised is come."),
("p", "The word “church” does not stand in the chapters that carry the judgments, the return and the marriage. In the chapters read for this volume it stands in the first and third chapters, where it names the assemblies addressed (Revelation 1:4, 11, 20; 3:1, 6, 7, 13, 14, 22), and in the closing words, “the churches” (Revelation 22:16). It does not occur in chapters 17, 18, 19 or 21. A reading that makes the wife of 19:7 the church must supply a word the chapter does not use, in a book where the word is used when it is meant."),
("sub", "7.3 The garments"),
("p", "The wife “hath made herself ready,” and the next verse says how: “to her was granted that she should be arrayed in fine linen, clean and white: for the fine linen is the righteousness of saints” (Revelation 19:8). Two things are said at once. She prepared herself, and the linen was granted her. The same two garments clothe the wife in the prophets. Ezekiel describes the LORD’s gifts to the girl he took: “I girded thee about with fine linen” and “thy raiment was of fine linen, and silk, and broidered work” (Ezekiel 16:10, 13). Isaiah’s Zion rejoices because the LORD “hath covered me with the robe of righteousness, as a bridegroom decketh himself with ornaments, and as a bride adorneth herself with her jewels” (Isaiah 61:10). Revelation 19:8 names the linen and the righteousness together, as the prophets do, and the reader is not asked to look elsewhere for the wife’s dress."),
("sub", "7.4 The supper and the angel’s word"),
("p", "The angel then speaks to John: “Write, Blessed are they which are called unto the marriage supper of the Lamb” (Revelation 19:9). The blessing is on those who are called. They are called to the supper and not to the marriage. The wife is the one who is wed, and the called are the ones who are blessed to sit at the table. Chapter 12 takes up who they are."),
("p", "When John falls to worship, the angel refuses and says, “I am thy fellowservant, and of thy brethren that have the testimony of Jesus: worship God: for the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy” (Revelation 19:10). The last clause ties the scene to the prophets. The testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy, and the marriage that has been announced is the marriage the prophets bore witness to. The setting is the setting of fulfilled prophecy and not of a new revelation about a different people."),
("tbl", "The table follows the verses clause by clause, with the prophets beside them.", [
    ("Rev 19:2", "“he hath judged the great whore, which did corrupt the earth with her fornication”", "The harlot is judged first"),
    ("Rev 19:6", "“Alleluia: for the Lord God omnipotent reigneth”", "The reign is the setting of the marriage"),
    ("Rev 19:7", "“the marriage of the Lamb is come, and his wife hath made herself ready”", "The word is wife, and the husband is the Lamb"),
    ("Rev 19:8", "“arrayed in fine linen, clean and white: for the fine linen is the righteousness of saints”", "Linen and righteousness are both named"),
    ("Ezek 16:13", "“thy raiment was of fine linen, and silk, and broidered work”", "The wife of Ezekiel is clothed in fine linen"),
    ("Isa 61:10", "“he hath covered me with the robe of righteousness”", "The wife of Isaiah is clothed in righteousness"),
    ("Rev 19:9", "“Blessed are they which are called unto the marriage supper of the Lamb”", "The called are blessed, and they are at the supper"),
    ("Rev 19:10", "“the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy”", "The scene belongs to the prophets’ testimony"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "7.5 What this chapter does not say"),
("bul", [
    "It does not say when the marriage supper is held in relation to the return in 19:11. The chapter announces the marriage and goes on to the war, and the volume does not add a date.",
    "It does not identify “the great whore” beyond what the chapter says of her judgment. The point is that the wife and the whore are contrasted.",
    "It does not say that “saints” in 19:8 names the Body. The word is used in the Scriptures for the holy ones of many times and places, and this chapter gives the wife’s linen a meaning in the prophets.",
  ]),
("call", "Where the argument stands", "The marriage of the Lamb is announced after the harlot’s judgment, in the setting of fulfilled prophecy. The wife is arrayed in the linen and righteousness that the prophets give her, the called are blessed as guests of the supper, and the chapter never calls the wife a church."),
]
